WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.957
The takeaway is simple.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:07.994
By training a neural network to emulate the slow
kinematics physics of JAM,

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:12.031
SKiNN achieves roughly a three-hundred-fold speedup
at sub-percent accuracy in the region that matters.

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:16.068
This removes the main computational bottleneck and
makes it feasible to jointly model

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:20.105
gravitational lensing together with spatially
resolved kinematics,

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:24.142
which corrects for the largest source of
uncertainty in measuring the Hubble constant.

00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:28.179
More broadly,

00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:32.216
the strategy of swapping an expensive but
calculable model piece for a trained neural network,

00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:36.253
while keeping the surrounding physics intact,

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:40.290
likely transfers to many other scientific modeling
problems.
